== Picard groups <sec:picard-groups>

Many of the results in this section are due to Fröhlich [@bib:Frohlich1973] (see
also Bass [@bib:Bass1968]). Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $Lambda$ any ring
(with unity element $1_Lambda$), and let $c(Lambda)$ denote the center of
$Lambda$. We have called $Lambda$ an _$R$-algebra_ if there is a ring
homomorphism $R -> c(Lambda)$ with $1_R -> 1_Lambda$. In this case, all
$Lambda$-modules may be viewed as $R$-modules. Since every ring is a
$bold(Z)$-algebra, there is no loss of generality in considering algebras rather
than rings.

Let $Lambda$, $Delta$ be $R$-algebras. We shall say that a bimodule
$lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ is _over $R$_ #idx("bimodule over R")if
$
  r m = m r quad "for all" r in R, m in M,
$
that is, $(r 1_Lambda) m = m (r 1_Delta)$ for all $r, m$. A bimodule
$lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ (over $R$) is _invertible_ #idx(
  "invertible bimodule",
)if #source(329)there exist a bimodule $lmodule(Delta, N)_Lambda$ (over $R$),
and a Morita context @def:morita-context, which give a Morita equivalence (over
$R$) of the rings $Lambda$, $Delta$. We call $N$ the _inverse_ of $M$. As shown
in §~@sec:morita-correspondence, the bimodule isomorphism class $(M)$ uniquely
determines the inverse class $(N)$; indeed, we have $(N) = (M^(-1))$, where
$M^(-1)$ is the bimodule given by
$
  lmodule(Delta, (M^(-1)))_Lambda
  = Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta, lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)_Lambda)
  tilde.equiv Hom_Delta (lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta, lmodule(Delta, Delta)_Delta).
$ <eq:inverse-bimodule>

We may restate the definition of invertibility in the following explicit form:
#idx("invertible bimodule")the bimodule $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ (over $R$) is
invertible (over $R$) if there exist a bimodule $lmodule(Delta, N)_Lambda$ (over
$R$) and bimodule isomorphisms
$
  M times.o_Delta N tilde.equiv Lambda, quad
  N times.o_Lambda M tilde.equiv Delta,
$ <eq:invertible-isomorphisms>
making the following diagrams commute:
$
  #invertibility-squares
$ <eq:invertible-associativity>

Let us recall from §~@sec:morita-correspondence that every invertible bimodule
$lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ is a progenerator for both of the categories
$lmodule(Lambda, cal(M))$ and $cal(M)_Delta$. Furthermore,
$
  Lambda tilde.equiv Hom_Delta (M, M), quad Delta tilde.equiv Hom_Lambda (M, M),
$ <eq:invertible-endomorphisms>
where both $Delta$ and $Hom_Lambda (M, M)$ are viewed as rings of right
operators on $M$. Conversely, progenerators yield Morita equivalences.

#definition(kind: "Definitions")[
  + Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra. The _Picard group of $Lambda$ relative to
    $R$_, #idx("Picard group", target: [@def:picard-group])denoted by
    $Pic_R Lambda$, is the multiplicative group consisting of all bimodule
    isomorphism classes $(M)$ of invertible bimodules
    $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Lambda$ over $R$. Multiplication is defined by the
    formula $(M)(M') = (M times.o_Lambda M')$. The class $(Lambda)$ is the
    identity element of $Pic_R Lambda$, and inverses are given by
    $(M)^(-1) = (M^(-1))$ with $M^(-1)$ defined as in @eq:inverse-bimodule,
    using $Lambda$ in place of $Delta$.
  + Let $C = c(Lambda)$, and view $Lambda$ as a $C$-algebra. We define#idx(
      "Picent",
    )
    $
      Picent Lambda = Pic_C Lambda,
    $
    so $Picent Lambda$ is the subgroup of $Pic_bold(Z) Lambda$ consisting of all
    classes of invertible bimodules $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Lambda$ such that
    $
      c m = m c quad "for all" c in C, m in M.
    $ <eq:picent-condition>
] <def:picard-group>

We remark that the groups $Pic_R Lambda$ and $Picent Lambda$ need not be
commutative. The group $Pic_bold(Z) Lambda$ is called the _Picard group_ of
$Lambda$, and the subscript $bold(Z)$ is usually omitted. As we shall see below
when we restrict our attention to the case where $Lambda$ is an order in a
semisimple algebra $A$ over a field, the group #source(330)$Picent
Lambda$ is precisely the group of invertible two-sided $Lambda$-ideals in $A$,
modulo principal ideals generated by units in $c(A)$. From this standpoint, the
group $Picent Lambda$ is a natural object of investigation.

In Exercise~@exc:morita-centers we have already noted that Morita equivalent
rings have isomorphic centers. Let us give this isomorphism explicitly:

#theorem[
  Let $Lambda, Delta$ be $R$-algebras, and let $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ be an
  invertible bimodule over $R$. Then $M$ determines an $R$-isomorphism
  $phi : c(Lambda) tilde.equiv c(Delta)$ as follows: for each $c in c(Lambda)$,
  $phi(c)$ is the unique element of $c(Delta)$ such that
  $
    c dot m = m dot phi(c) quad "for all" m in M.
  $ <eq:center-isomorphism>
  Taking $R = bold(Z)$, it follows that Morita equivalent rings have isomorphic
  centers.
] <th:morita-centers>

#proof[
  Since $M$ is invertible, @eq:invertible-endomorphisms shows that each element
  of $Lambda$ (or of $Delta$) is completely determined by its action on $M$. For
  each $c in C$, the map $m -> c m$, $m in M$, is a left $Lambda$-endomorphism
  of $M$. Hence by @eq:invertible-endomorphisms there is a unique
  $phi(c) in Delta$ such that @eq:center-isomorphism holds true. But then for
  all $x in Delta$ and $m in M$,
  $
    m(phi(c) x) = (c m) x = c(m x) = (m x) phi(c) = m(x phi(c)).
  $
  Thus $phi(c)$ commutes with each $x in Delta$, so $phi(c) in c(Delta)$. It is
  easily verified that $phi$ is an $R$-isomorphism of rings, and the theorem is
  proved.
]

Let us show next that Morita equivalent rings have isomorphic Picard groups.

#theorem[
  If the $R$-algebras $Lambda, Delta$ are Morita equivalent over $R$, then
  $Pic_R Lambda tilde.equiv Pic_R Delta$. If the rings $Lambda, Lambda'$ are
  Morita equivalent, then $Picent Lambda tilde.equiv Picent Lambda'$.
] <th:morita-picard>

#proof[
  Let $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Delta$ be an invertible bimodule over $R$. It is
  easily verified that the map
  $
    (X) -> (M^(-1) times.o_Lambda X times.o_Lambda M), quad
    (X) in Pic_R Lambda,
  $
  gives an isomorphism $Pic_R Lambda tilde.equiv Pic_R Delta$. (We should remark
  that this isomorphism may depend on the choice of $M$.)

  Secondly, let the invertible bimodule $lmodule(Lambda, N)_(Lambda')$ determine
  an isomorphism $phi : C tilde.equiv C'$ as in @th:morita-centers, where
  $C = c(Lambda)$, $C' = c(Lambda')$. Then
  $
    c dot n = n dot phi(c) quad "for all" c in C, n in N.
  $
  For $(X) in Picent Lambda$ we have $c x = x c$ for all $c in C$, $x in X$. Let
  us deduce from this that
  $(N^(-1) times.o_Lambda X times.o_Lambda N) in Picent Lambda'$. Each element
  of $C'$ is of the form $phi(c)$, for some $c in C$. Then we have
  #source(331)
  $
    (n' times.o x times.o n) phi(c) & = n' times.o x times.o c n
                                      = n' c times.o x times.o n \
                                    & = phi(c) (n' times.o x times.o n),
                                      "for all" n' times.o x times.o n
                                      in N^(-1) times.o X times.o N.
  $
  The remaining details of the proof are obvious. A supplement to this theorem
  is given in Exercise~@exc:picard-azumaya.
]

Now let $Aut_R Lambda$ be the group of all $R$-automorphisms of the $R$-algebra
$Lambda$. For each $u in u(Lambda)$, let $i_u$ be the inner automorphism #idx(
  "inner automorphism",
)defined by
$
  i_u (x) = u x u^(-1), quad x in Lambda.
$ <eq:inner-automorphism>
The _group of inner automorphisms_ of $Lambda$ is defined as
$
  In Lambda = {i_u : u in u(Lambda)}.
$
Since
$
  alpha dot i_u dot alpha^(-1) = i_(alpha u), quad alpha in Aut_R Lambda, quad
  u in u(Lambda),
$
it follows that $In Lambda$ is a normal subgroup of $Aut_R Lambda$. We set
$
  Out_R Lambda = Aut_R Lambda slash In Lambda,
$ <eq:outer-automorphism-group>
the _outer automorphism group of $Lambda$ over $R$_.#idx(
  "outer automorphism group",
)

#definition[
  Let $Lambda, Delta$ be $R$-algebras, $lmodule(Lambda, X)_Delta$ a bimodule
  over $R$. Given any automorphisms $f in Aut_R Lambda$, $g in Aut_R Delta$, let
  $lmodule(f, X)_g$ be the bimodule (over $R$) having the same elements as $X$,
  but with the action of $Lambda$ "twisted" by $f$, that of $Delta$ by $g$. This
  means that
  $
    lambda compose x compose delta quad ("in" lmodule(f, X)_g)
    = f(lambda) dot x dot g(delta) quad ("in" X)
  $
  for each $x in X$, $lambda in Lambda$, $delta in Delta$. It is easily verified
  that
  $
    lmodule(f', (lmodule(f, X)_g))_(g') tilde.equiv lmodule(f f', X)_(g g')
    quad "as bimodules."
  $
] <def:twisted-bimodule>

#lemma[
  Consider the $R$-algebra $Lambda$ as a $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule. For each
  $f, g in Aut_R Lambda$, there is a $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule
  $lmodule(f, Lambda)_g$ over $R$. The following bimodule isomorphisms hold
  true:
  $
    lmodule(f, Lambda)_g tilde.equiv lmodule(h f, Lambda)_(h g)
    tilde.equiv lmodule(1, Lambda)_(f^(-1) g)
    tilde.equiv lmodule(g^(-1) f, Lambda)_1, quad
    lmodule(f', (lmodule(f, Lambda)_g))_(g')
    tilde.equiv lmodule(f f', Lambda)_(g g'), \
    lmodule(f, Lambda)_g times.o lmodule(f', Lambda)_(g')
    tilde.equiv lmodule(f, Lambda)_(g f'^(-1) g'), quad
    lmodule(1, Lambda)_g times.o lmodule(1, Lambda)_(g')
    tilde.equiv lmodule(1, Lambda)_(g g'), \
    lmodule(1, Lambda)_f times.o lmodule(f, Lambda)_1 tilde.equiv Lambda
    tilde.equiv lmodule(f, Lambda)_1 times.o lmodule(1, Lambda)_f,
  $
  where $times.o$ means $times.o_Lambda$.
] <lem:twisted-bimodules>

#proof[Exercise for the reader.]

#theorem[
  The map $omega_0 : Aut_R Lambda -> Pic_R Lambda$, defined by
  #source(332)
  $
    omega_0 (f) = (lmodule(1, Lambda)_f), quad f in Aut_R Lambda,
  $
  is a homomorphism of groups. We have $ker omega_0 = In Lambda$, the group of
  inner automorphisms of $Lambda$. Therefore $omega_0$ induces a
  _monomorphism_
  $
    omega : Out_R Lambda -> Pic_R Lambda,
  $
  where $Out_R Lambda$ is given by @eq:outer-automorphism-group.
] <th:automorphisms-picard>

#proof[
  Each $f in Aut_R Lambda$ gives rise to a $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule
  $lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$ over $R$, and by @lem:twisted-bimodules,
  $lmodule(f, Lambda)_1$ is an inverse for $lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$. Thus
  $omega_0 (f) = (lmodule(1, Lambda)_f) in Pic_R Lambda$. Further, for
  $f, g in Aut_R Lambda$,
  $
    omega_0 (f g) = (lmodule(1, Lambda)_(f g))
    = (lmodule(1, Lambda)_f)(lmodule(1, Lambda)_g)
  $
  by @lem:twisted-bimodules, so $omega_0$ is a homomorphism.

  Let us prove that $ker omega_0 = In Lambda$. We have $omega_0 (f) = 1$ if and
  only if there exists a bimodule isomorphism
  $theta : Lambda tilde.equiv lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$. This occurs if and only if
  there is a bijection $theta : Lambda -> Lambda$ such that
  $
    theta(a x b) = a dot theta(x) dot f(b) quad "for all" a, b, x in Lambda.
  $ <eq:bimodule-twist-condition>

  Now let $u in u(Lambda)$, and let $f$ be the inner automorphism $i_u$ defined
  by @eq:inner-automorphism. Set $theta(x) = x u^(-1)$, $x in Lambda$. Condition
  @eq:bimodule-twist-condition becomes
  $
    (a x b) u^(-1) = a dot x u^(-1) dot i_u (b) = a dot x u^(-1) dot u b u^(-1),
  $
  which is always true. Therefore $Lambda tilde.equiv lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$
  whenever $f$ is inner, and thus $In Lambda subset ker omega_0$.

  Conversely, let $theta : Lambda -> Lambda$ be a bijection such that
  @eq:bimodule-twist-condition holds, and set $u = theta(1)$. Then
  $
    Lambda = theta(Lambda) = theta(Lambda dot 1) = Lambda theta(1) = Lambda u,
  $
  and likewise $Lambda = u Lambda$, so $u in u(Lambda)$. Taking $x = b = 1$ in
  @eq:bimodule-twist-condition, we obtain
  $
    theta(a) = a u quad "for all" a in Lambda.
  $
  Next choose $a = x = 1$ in @eq:bimodule-twist-condition, whence
  $
    theta(b) = u dot f(b) quad "for all" b in Lambda.
  $
  Therefore
  $
    a u = theta(a) = u dot f(a) quad "for all" a in Lambda,
  $
  whence $f = i_(u^(-1)) in In Lambda$. This completes the proof that
  $ker omega_0 = In Lambda$, and the remaining assertion in the theorem is now
  obvious.
]

The preceding discussion is useful in considering the one-sided module structure
of invertible bimodules. The following result is fundamental:

#theorem[
  #source(333)Let $(X), (Y) in Pic_R Lambda$. Then
  $lmodule(Lambda, X) tilde.equiv lmodule(Lambda, Y)$ if and only if
  $(Y) in (X) dot im omega$, that is, if and only if
  $Y tilde.equiv lmodule(1, X)_f$ (as bimodules) for some $f in Aut_R Lambda$.
] <th:left-isomorphic-invertible>

#proof[
  For each $f in Aut_R Lambda$, there is a $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule
  isomorphism
  $
    lmodule(1, X)_f = X times.o_Lambda lmodule(1, Lambda)_f.
  $
  Hence $(Y) in (X) dot im omega$ if and only if $Y tilde.equiv lmodule(1, X)_f$
  for some $f$. It is clear that any bimodule isomorphism
  $Y tilde.equiv lmodule(1, X)_f$ is also a left $Lambda$-isomorphism
  $lmodule(Lambda, Y) tilde.equiv lmodule(Lambda, X)$.

  Conversely, let $h : X tilde.equiv Y$ be a left $Lambda$-isomorphism. Then $h$
  induces an isomorphism $h^*$ of endomorphism rings:
  $
    h^* : Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, Y), lmodule(Lambda, Y))
    tilde.equiv Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, X), lmodule(Lambda, X)),
  $
  given by $h^* (phi) = h^(-1) phi h$, for
  $phi in Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, Y), lmodule(Lambda, Y))$. Since
  $lmodule(Lambda, X)_Lambda$ is invertible, each element in
  $Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, X), lmodule(Lambda, X))$ is given by a right
  multiplication $a_r : x -> x a$, $x in X$, for some uniquely determined
  element $a in Lambda$. Likewise, every element of
  $Hom_Lambda (lmodule(Lambda, Y), lmodule(Lambda, Y))$ is of the form
  $b_r : y -> y b$, $y in Y$, for some uniquely determined $b in Lambda$.
  Therefore each $b in Lambda$ determines a unique $a in Lambda$ such that
  $h^* (b_r) = a_r$, that is,
  $
    h^(-1) b_r h = a_r quad "on" quad X.
  $
  This gives
  $
    (h^(-1) b_r h) x = a_r x = x a quad "for all" quad x in X,
  $
  that is,
  $
    (h x) b = h(x a) quad "for all" quad x in X.
  $
  Setting $a = f(b)$, we obtain the identity
  $
    (h x) b = h(x dot f(b)) quad "for all" quad x in X, quad b in Lambda.
  $
  It follows readily that $f in Aut_R Lambda$, and that the map
  $h : lmodule(1, X)_f -> Y$ given by $x -> h(x)$, $x in lmodule(1, X)_f$, is a
  bimodule isomorphism. We have thus shown that if
  $lmodule(Lambda, X) tilde.equiv lmodule(Lambda, Y)$, then
  $Y tilde.equiv lmodule(1, X)_f$ (as bimodules) for some $f in Aut_R Lambda$.
  This completes the proof of the theorem.
]

Before proceeding with our investigation of $Picent Lambda$, let us briefly
consider the relation between $Picent Lambda$ and $Pic_R Lambda$ when $Lambda$
is an $R$-algebra. Let $C = c(Lambda)$. By @th:morita-centers, each invertible
$lmodule(Lambda, M)_Lambda$ over $R$ determines an $R$-automorphism $Phi_M$ of
$C$, according to the condition that for each $c in C$,
$
  Phi_M (c) dot m = m dot c quad "for all" quad m in M.
$
It is clear that $Phi_M$ depends only upon the bimodule isomorphism class of
$M$.

Let us calculate $Phi_M$ for the case where $M = lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$,
$f in Aut_R Lambda$. We show that
$
  M = lmodule(1, Lambda)_f ==> Phi_M "is the restriction of" f "to" C.
$ <eq:phi-of-twisted>
#source(334)Indeed, let $c in C$, and let us verify that
$
  f(c) dot m = m dot c quad "for all" m in M.
$
Since $M = lmodule(1, Lambda)_f$, this condition becomes
$
  f(c) dot lambda = lambda dot f(c) quad "for all" lambda in Lambda,
$
which certainly holds true since $f(c) in C$. This establishes
@eq:phi-of-twisted, which we shall need for the next result.

#theorem[
  For any $R$-algebra $Lambda$ with center $C$, there is an exact sequence
  $
    1 -> Picent Lambda -> Pic_R Lambda stretch(->)^Phi Aut_R C.
  $ <eq:picent-pic-aut>
  If $Lambda$ is commutative, then the sequence
  $
    1 -> Picent Lambda -> Pic_R Lambda stretch(->)^Phi
    Aut_R Lambda -> 1
  $ <eq:picent-pic-aut-split>
  is split exact.
] <th:picent-pic-sequence>

#proof[
  Let $(M), (N) in Pic_R Lambda$, and set $f = Phi_M$, $g = Phi_N$. Then for
  each $c in C$, we have
  $
    f(c) dot m = m c, quad g(c) dot n = n c, quad "for all" m in M, n in N.
  $
  Therefore
  $
    (m times.o n) c = m dot g(c) times.o n = f(g(c))(m times.o n) quad
    "for all" m times.o n in M times.o_Lambda N.
  $
  This shows that $Phi_(M times.o N) = Phi_M compose Phi_N$, and thus $Phi$ is a
  homomorphism. Furthermore, $Phi_M = 1$ if and only if $c m = m c$ for all
  $m in M$, $c in C$, that is, if and only if $(M) in Picent Lambda$. This
  completes the proof that the sequence @eq:picent-pic-aut is exact, once we
  observe that the mapping of $Picent Lambda$ into $Pic_R Lambda$ is just the
  inclusion map.

  Now suppose that $Lambda$ is commutative, and let $omega_0$ be the map defined
  in @th:automorphisms-picard. Thus for $f in Aut_R Lambda$, we put
  $omega_0 (f) = (lmodule(1, Lambda)_f) in Pic_R Lambda$. Since $Lambda$ is
  commutative, it follows from @eq:phi-of-twisted that $Phi omega_0 (f) = f$.
  This shows that $Phi omega_0$ is the identity map on $Aut_R Lambda$, whence by
  §~@ss:modules the map $Phi$ is epic, and the exact sequence
  @eq:picent-pic-aut-split is split. This completes the proof of the theorem.
]

#remark(kind: "Remarks", numbered: false)[
  + In some cases one can describe explicitly the image of the map $Phi$
    occurring in @eq:picent-pic-aut. Suppose for example that
    $A = dsum_(i=1)^t A_i$ is semisimple, where for each $i$ the simple
    component $A_i$ has skewfield part $D_i$ and center $K_i$. Then
    $c(A) = dsum K_i$, and each $f in Aut_K c(A)$ must permute the ${K_i}$.
    Hence $f$ is completely determined by some permutation $pi$ of the set
    ${1, dots, t}$, together with a collection of $K$-isomorphisms
    $f_i : K_i tilde.equiv K_(pi(i))$, #source(335)$1 <= i <= t$, where $f_i$ is
    the restriction of $f$ to $K_i$. Then
    $Picent A tilde.equiv product Picent A_i$ by
    Exercise~@exc:picent-direct-sum, and the exact sequence @eq:picent-pic-aut
    becomes#footnote[To clarify the later discussion, we write $Phi'$ rather
      than $Phi$.]
    $
      1 -> Picent A -> Pic_K A stretch(->)^(Phi') Aut_K c(A).
    $
    Each $(X) in Pic_K A$ yields a Morita equivalence of the $K$-algebra $A$
    with itself. Using this fact, Fröhlich [@bib:Frohlich1973] proves that the
    image of $Phi'$ consists precisely of those elements $f in Aut_K c(A)$ such
    that for $1 <= i <= t$, the isomorphism $f_i : K_i tilde.equiv K_(pi(i))$
    can be extended to an isomorphism $D_i tilde.equiv D_(pi(i))$. We shall see
    in @th:picent-semisimple that $Picent A = 1$, and thus
    $Pic_K A tilde.equiv im Phi'$ in the present case.
  + Now let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, and let $Lambda$
    be a maximal $R$-order in a separable $K$-algebra $A$. Keep the above
    notation. There is a group isomorphism
    $
      Aut_R c(Lambda) tilde.equiv Aut_K c(A),
    $
    given by extending each $f in Aut_R c(Lambda)$ to an $f' in Aut_K c(A)$. By
    using the result that any two maximal orders must be Morita equivalent (see
    Exercise~@exc:maximal-orders-morita-equivalent), Fröhlich shows that
    $
      f in im Phi <=> f' in im Phi',
    $
    where $Phi$ is the map occurring in @eq:picent-pic-aut. Thus $im Phi$ is
    known also for the case of maximal orders.
  + Keep the notation of (i), and consider the exact sequence
    $
      1 -> Picent A_1 -> Pic_K A_1 stretch(-->)^(Phi'') Aut_K K_1.
    $
    By (i), $im Phi''$ consists precisely of those $K$-automorphisms $f$ of
    $K_1$ which can be extended to a $K$-automorphism of $A_1$.
]

#theorem(major: true)[
  Let $A$ be any semisimple artinian ring. Then
  $
    Picent A = 1.
  $
] <th:picent-semisimple>

#proof[
  We may write $A = dsum_(i=1)^t A_i$, where each $A_i$ is a simple artinian
  ring. Then
  $
    Picent A tilde.equiv product_(i=1)^t Picent A_i
  $
  by Exercise~@exc:picent-direct-sum, so we need only show that each
  $Picent A_i = 1$. Changing notation, let $A$ be a simple artinian ring with
  center $K$, that is, $A$ is a central simple $K$-algebra. Let $V$ be a simple
  left $A$-module, $D = Hom_A (V, V) =$ skewfield part of $A$, and view $V$ as a
  bimodule $lmodule(A, V)_D$ of right $D$-dimension $n$. Then
  $
    A = Hom_D (V, V) tilde.equiv M_n (D).
  $

  #source(336)Now let $(X) in Picent A$. Then
  $lmodule(A, X) tilde.equiv V^((k))$ for some $k$, and therefore
  $
    Hom_A (lmodule(A, X), lmodule(A, X)) tilde.equiv Hom_A (V^((k)), V^((k)))
    tilde.equiv M_k (D),
  $
  where each endomorphism ring is viewed as right operator domain. But
  $Hom_A (lmodule(A, X), lmodule(A, X)) tilde.equiv A$ since $lmodule(A, X)_A$
  is invertible. Therefore $A tilde.equiv M_k (D)$, whence $k = n$. This proves
  that $X tilde.equiv A$ as left $A$-modules. Hence by
  @th:left-isomorphic-invertible, there exists an $f in Aut_K A$ such that
  $X tilde.equiv lmodule(1, A)_f$ as bimodules. But $f$ is an inner automorphism
  of $A$, by the Skolem–Noether Theorem. Hence $lmodule(1, A)_f tilde.equiv A$
  as bimodules, by @th:automorphisms-picard. This shows that $(X) = 1$ in
  $Picent A$, and completes the proof of the theorem. One may also prove that
  $Picent A = 1$ by using Exercise~@exc:picard-azumaya, since $A$ is an Azumaya
  $K$-algebra, and since $Pic_K K = 1$.
]

#theorem(major: true)[
  Let $Lambda$ be a commutative ring such that $Lambda slash rad Lambda$ is a
  direct sum of fields. Then $Picent Lambda = 1$. This holds in particular when
  $Lambda$ is a commutative algebra over a local ring $R$, and $Lambda$ is
  finitely generated as $R$-module.
] <th:picent-commutative-semilocal>

#proof[
  Set $N = rad Lambda$, $overline(Lambda) = Lambda slash N$. Clearly
  $overline(Lambda)$ is a commutative ring such that $rad overline(Lambda) = 0$.
  Thus $overline(Lambda)$ is a direct sum of fields if and only if
  $overline(Lambda)$ is an artinian ring.

  Any left $Lambda$-module $X$ may be viewed as a bimodule
  $lmodule(Lambda, X)_Lambda$ over $Lambda$, by defining
  $x dot lambda = lambda x$, $lambda in Lambda$, $x in X$. All bimodules over
  $Lambda$ arise in this way. For each $X$, let $overline(X) = X slash N X$. We
  claim that there is a homomorphism $Picent Lambda -> Picent overline(Lambda)$,
  given by $(X) -> (overline(X))$. Indeed, given a Morita context where
  $X times.o Y tilde.equiv Lambda$, $Y times.o X tilde.equiv Lambda$, we obtain
  a new context $overline(X) times.o overline(Y) -> overline(Lambda)$,
  $overline(Y) times.o overline(X) -> overline(Lambda)$ in which both maps are
  epic, and hence $(overline(X)) in Picent overline(Lambda)$.

  Now $Picent overline(Lambda) = 1$ since $overline(Lambda)$ is semisimple
  artinian, and hence for each $(X) in Picent Lambda$ there exists a
  $overline(Lambda)$-isomorphism $f : overline(X) tilde.equiv overline(Lambda)$.
  In the diagram
  $
    #lifted-pair(
      ($X$, $Lambda$),
      ($overline(X)$, $overline(Lambda),$),
      ($phi$, $psi$),
      ($f$, $f^(-1)$),
    )
  $
  we can find $Lambda$-homomorphisms $phi, psi$ which lift $f, f^(-1)$
  respectively, since $X$ and $Lambda$ are $Lambda$-projective. Then
  $phi psi(a) - a in N$ for all $a in Lambda$, whence
  $Lambda = phi psi(Lambda) + N$. It follows that $phi psi(Lambda) = Lambda$,
  and so $phi$ is epic. Therefore $X = ker phi plus.o X_1$, where
  $phi : X_1 tilde.equiv Lambda$. Consequently $overline(ker phi) = 0$ in
  $overline(X)$, whence $ker phi = 0$. This proves that $X tilde.equiv Lambda$,
  as desired. The second assertion in the theorem follows from the first, by use
  of @th:radical-local-algebra.
]

For the remainder of this section let $R$ be a domain with quotient field $K$,
and let $Lambda$ be an $R$-order in a $K$-algebra $A$. If $M, N$ are two-sided
$Lambda$-submodules of $A$ such that $M N = N M = Lambda$, we call $M$ an
_invertible_ $Lambda$-ideal #idx("invertible ideal")#idx(
  "ideal",
  "invertible",
)in $A$, and $N$ its _inverse_. Then $K M = K N = A$. By
Exercise~@exc:invertible-ideal-bimodule, $M$ is an invertible #source(
  337,
)$(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule, and hence $(M) in Picent Lambda$. The same
Exercise shows that $(M)^(-1) = (N)$ in $Picent Lambda$. Let us denote by
$I(Lambda)$ the multiplicative group of invertible $Lambda$-ideals in $A$, with
multiplication performed within $A$. If $C = c(Lambda)$, then obviously
$c(A) = K C$. We prove

#theorem[
  There is an exact sequence
  $
    1 -> u(C) -> u(K C) stretch(->)^rho I(Lambda) stretch(->)^sigma
    Picent Lambda stretch(->)^tau Picent A,
  $
  where $rho(u) = Lambda u$, $sigma(M) = (M)$, $tau(X) = (K X)$.
] <th:picent-ideal-sequence>

#proof[
  Exactness at $u(K C)$ is clear. By Exercise~@exc:bimodule-isomorphic-to-order,
  $ker sigma = im rho$. Finally, $ker tau = im sigma$ by
  Exercise~@exc:picent-kernel-ideal.
]

#corollary[
  If $A$ is semisimple, then
  $
    Picent Lambda tilde.equiv I(Lambda) slash {Lambda u : u in u(K C)}.
  $
] <cor:picent-ideal-classes>

This corollary justifies to some extent our emphasis on the group
$Picent Lambda$, rather than $Pic_R Lambda$. It shows that $Picent Lambda$
occurs in a natural way as a class group of invertible $Lambda$-ideals in $A$.

Let us define #idx("Autcent")#idx("Outcent")
$
  Autcent Lambda = Aut_C Lambda, quad
  Outcent Lambda = Out_C Lambda
  = Aut_C Lambda slash In Lambda,
$
where $C = c(Lambda)$. By @th:automorphisms-picard, there is a monomorphism
$
  omega : Outcent Lambda -> Picent Lambda, quad
  f -> (lmodule(1, Lambda)_f).
$
Now define the _normalizer_ #idx("normalizer of an order")of $Lambda$ in $A$ as
$
  N(Lambda) = {x in u(A) : x Lambda x^(-1) = Lambda}.
$

#theorem[
  Let $A$ be a semisimple $K$-algebra. There is a commutative diagram
  $
    #normalizer-triangle
  $
  where for $x in N(Lambda)$,
  $
    rho(x) = i_x, quad omega'(x) = (Lambda x),
  $
  and $i_x$ is the inner automorphism $lambda -> x lambda x^(-1)$,
  $lambda in Lambda$. The map $rho$ is an isomorphism, and $omega'$ a
  monomorphism.
] <th:normalizer-outcent>

#proof[
  Each $f in Aut_C Lambda$ extends to an $tilde(f) in Aut_(K C) A$. By the
  Skolem–Noether #source(338)Theorem @cor:automorphisms-inner, $tilde(f) = i_x$
  for some $x in u(A)$. This shows that $rho$ is epic. The remaining part of the
  proof is left as exercise for the reader.
]

#corollary[
  Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring.
  + If $Lambda$ is a maximal $R$-order in a separable $K$-algebra $A$, then the
    maps $rho, omega, omega'$ in @th:normalizer-outcent are isomorphisms.
  + If $Lambda = M_n (R)$, then $N(Lambda) = u(Lambda) dot u(K)$.
] <cor:normalizer-dvr>

#proof[
  (i) Let $(X) in Picent Lambda$. By @cor:picent-ideal-classes we may assume
  that $X$ is a two-sided $Lambda$-ideal in $A$, such that $K X = A$. Then
  $lmodule(Lambda, X) tilde.equiv lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)$ by
  @th:separable-maximal-principal, and hence $(X) in im omega$ by
  @th:left-isomorphic-invertible. This proves that $omega$ is epic, and
  establishes assertion (i) of the corollary.

  (ii) When $Lambda = M_n (R)$ we have $Picent Lambda tilde.equiv Picent R = 1$,
  and so the second assertion of the corollary follows from the first.
]

One of our ultimate aims is to evaluate Picent of a maximal order. As a first
step in this direction, we treat the complete local case.

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a complete discrete valuation ring, and let $Lambda$ be a maximal
  $R$-order in a central simple $K$-algebra $A$ with skewfield part $D$. Then
  $
    Picent Lambda tilde.equiv bold(Z) slash e bold(Z),
  $
  where $e = e(D slash K)$ is the ramification index of $D$ over $K$. If the
  discriminant $d(Lambda slash R)$ equals $R$, then $Picent Lambda = 1$.
] <th:picent-complete-local>

#proof[
  Let $pi$ be a prime element of $R$, and let $pi_D$ be a prime element of
  $Delta$, where $Delta$ is the unique maximal $R$-order in $D$ (see
  §~@sec:uniqueness-of-maximal-orders). Then
  $
    pi Delta = (pi_D Delta)^e, quad rad Lambda = pi_D Lambda
  $
  by §~@sec:ramification-index-inertial-degree and @cor:complete-local-radical.
  Therefore $pi Lambda = (rad Lambda)^e$. But by
  @th:complete-local-maximal-orders, $I(Lambda)$ is the infinite cyclic group
  generated by $rad Lambda$. Since
  $
    Picent Lambda tilde.equiv I(Lambda) slash {pi^k Lambda : k in bold(Z)}
  $
  by @cor:picent-ideal-classes, we obtain
  $Picent Lambda tilde.equiv bold(Z) slash e bold(Z)$, as desired.

  If $e > 1$, then $(rad Lambda)^(e-1)$ divides the different
  $frak(D)(Lambda slash R)$, by Step 2 of the proof of
  @th:different-ramification. Therefore $pi^(e-1) R$ divides the discriminant
  $d(Lambda slash R)$. Hence if $d(Lambda slash R) = R$, then necessarily
  $e = 1$ and $Picent Lambda = 1$. This completes the proof.
]

In order to generalize the preceding theorem to the case where $R$ is an
arbitrary Dedekind domain, we now prove an important result concerning the
relation between global and local Picard groups. This result will be valid for
_all_ orders, whether or not they are maximal.

#theorem(major: true)[
  #source(339)Let $Lambda$ be any $R$-order in a separable $K$-algebra $A$,
  where $R$ is a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, and let
  $C = c(Lambda)$. For each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$, let $Lambda_P$ denote the
  $P$-adic completion of $Lambda$. Then $Picent Lambda_P = 1$ almost everywhere,
  and there is an exact sequence
  $
    1 -> Picent C stretch(->)^tau Picent Lambda stretch(->)^(tau')
    product_P Picent Lambda_P -> 1.
  $ <eq:picent-local-global>
  The map $tau$ is given by
  $
    tau(L) = (L times.o_C Lambda), quad (L) in Picent C.
  $
] <th:picent-local-global>

#proof[
  _Step 1._ For each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$, let $R_((P))$ denote the
  _localization_ of $R$ at $P$ (as defined in §~@sec:localization), and let
  $R_P$ be the $P$-adic completion of $R$. We may identify $R_P$ with the
  completion of the discrete valuation ring $R_((P))$ relative to its maximal
  ideal $P dot R_((P))$. As pointed out in §~@ss:completions, for each finitely
  generated $R$-module $M$, we may form its $P$-adic completion $M_P$ by first
  forming the localization $M_((P)) = R_((P)) times.o_R M$, and then passing to
  completions:
  $
    M_P = R_P times.o_(R_((P))) M_((P)) tilde.equiv R_P times.o_R M.
  $

  Since $A$ is a separable $K$-algebra, we know from @cor:maximal-orders-exist
  that there exists a maximal $R$-order in $A$, say $Gamma$. By
  @cor:maximal-order-completions, $Gamma_P$ is a maximal $R_P$-order in $A_P$.
  But $Lambda$ and $Gamma$ are a pair of full $R$-lattices in $A$, and so by
  Exercise~@exc:full-lattices-equal-ae we have $Lambda_((P)) = Gamma_((P))$ a.e.
  Therefore $Lambda_P = Gamma_P$ a.e., which proves that for almost all $P$,
  $Lambda_P$ is a maximal $R_P$-order in $A_P$.

  Now let $d(Lambda slash R)$ be the discriminant of $Lambda$ with respect to
  $R$ (see §~@sec:existence-of-maximal-orders,
  §~@sec:different-discriminant-global). Then $d(Lambda slash R)$ is a nonzero
  ideal of $R$, since $A$ is a separable $K$-algebra, and we have
  $
    d(Lambda_P slash R_P) = {d(Lambda slash R)}_P
  $
  by Exercise~@exc:discriminant-base-change. But ${d(Lambda slash R)}_P = R_P$
  whenever $P divides.not d(Lambda slash R)$. Since there are only finitely many
  $P$'s dividing $d(Lambda slash R)$, it follows that
  $d(Lambda_P slash R_P) = R_P$ a.e.

  _Step 2._ We have now shown that for all but a finite number of maximal ideals
  $P$ of $R$, we have
  $
    Lambda_P = "maximal" R_P"-order in" A_P, quad "and" quad
    d(Lambda_P slash R_P) = R_P.
  $ <eq:unramified-maximal-local>
  We claim that $Picent Lambda_P = 1$ whenever @eq:unramified-maximal-local
  holds true. To verify this, let us write $A_P = dsum A_i$, where each $A_i$ is
  a central simple algebra over a field $K_i$ containing $K_P$. Let $R_i$ be the
  integral closure of $R_P$ in $K_i$, so each $R_i$ is a complete discrete
  valuation ring. By @th:maximal-orders-components we have
  $Lambda_P = dsum Lambda_i$, where for each $i$, $Lambda_i$ is a maximal
  $R_i$-order in $A_i$. Since $d(Lambda_P slash R_P) = R_P$, it follows from
  Exercise~@exc:discriminant-components that $d(Lambda_i slash R_i) = R_i$ for
  each $i$. Hence each $Picent Lambda_i = 1$, by @th:picent-complete-local.
  Therefore
  $
    Picent Lambda_P tilde.equiv product Picent Lambda_i = 1
  $
  #source(340)by Exercise~@exc:picent-direct-sum. This shows that
  $Picent Lambda_P = 1$ a.e., as asserted.

  _Step 3._ We shall identify the set of left $C$-modules with the set of
  $(C, C)$-bimodules over $C$. For each left $C$-module $L$, the bimodule
  structure $lmodule(Lambda, Lambda)_Lambda$ permits us to view
  $L times.o_C Lambda$ as a $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodule over $C$. Explicitly we
  have
  $
    x(l times.o lambda) y = l times.o x lambda y, quad l in L, quad
    x, lambda, y in Lambda.
  $
  This construction may be visualized more easily as follows: given a
  $C$-submodule $L$ of $K C$, we may form the two-sided $Lambda$-submodule
  $L Lambda$ in $A$. Denote by $I(C)$ the group of invertible $C$-ideals in
  $K C$. Then for each $L in I(C)$, there is an isomorphism
  $
    L times.o_C Lambda tilde.equiv L Lambda quad "as" quad
    (Lambda, Lambda)"-bimodules."
  $
  Furthermore, if $L' in I(C)$ is such that $L L' = C$, then
  $
    (L Lambda)(L' Lambda) = (L' Lambda)(L Lambda) = Lambda.
  $
  Hence for each $L in I(C)$ we have $L Lambda in I(Lambda)$.

  Now let $(L) in Picent C$; by @cor:picent-ideal-classes, we may assume that
  $L in I(C)$, and the preceding discussion shows that
  $
    tau(L) = (L times.o_C Lambda) = (L Lambda) in Picent Lambda.
  $
  For $L_1, L_2 in I(C)$ we have $(L_1)(L_2) = (L_1 L_2)$ in $Picent C$, and
  hence
  $
    tau(L_1) tau(L_2) = (L_1 Lambda dot L_2 Lambda) = (L_1 L_2 dot Lambda)
    = tau(L_1 L_2).
  $
  Thus $tau$ is a homomorphism. We must still show that $tau$ is monic.

  For any bimodule $lmodule(Lambda, M)_Lambda$, let us set
  $
    M^Lambda = {m in M : lambda m = m lambda quad "for all" quad
      lambda in Lambda}.
  $
  Then $M^Lambda$ is a $C$-module, whose isomorphism class depends only upon the
  class $(M)$. Obviously
  $
    (M dotplus N)^Lambda = M^Lambda dotplus N^Lambda, quad Lambda^Lambda = C,
  $
  if $lmodule(Lambda, N)_Lambda$ is another bimodule. We now verify that for
  each $(L) in Picent C$,
  $
    {L times.o_C Lambda}^Lambda = L times.o_C C tilde.equiv L quad "as" quad
    C"-modules."
  $ <eq:invariants-of-extension>
  The formula is clearly valid when $L = C$, and hence also for every projective
  $C$-module $L$ by @rem:free-then-projective, and hence it holds true whenever
  $(L) in Picent C$.

  Suppose now that $(L) in Picent C$ is such that $tau(L) = 1$, that is,
  $(L times.o_C Lambda) = (Lambda)$. Then there are $C$-isomorphisms
  $
    L tilde.equiv (L times.o_C Lambda)^Lambda tilde.equiv Lambda^Lambda = C,
  $
  and therefore $(L) = 1$ in $Picent C$. This completes the proof that $tau$ is
  monic. Another proof of this fact is given in Exercise~@exc:tau-monic-direct.

  #source(341)_Step 4._ The map $tau'$ in @eq:picent-local-global is defined by
  setting
  $
    tau'(X) = product_P (X_P), quad (X) in Picent Lambda.
  $
  It is clear that for each $P$, the $(Lambda_P, Lambda_P)$-bimodule $X_P$ is
  invertible. Furthermore, $c x = x c$ for all $c in C$, $x in X$, since
  $(X) in Picent Lambda$. The same equality therefore holds for all $c in C_P$,
  $x in X_P$. But $C_P = c(Lambda_P)$ by Exercise~@exc:center-of-completion, and
  hence $(X_P) in Picent Lambda_P$. This shows that
  $im tau' subset product Picent Lambda_P$, and obviously $tau'$ is a
  homomorphism.

  We now prove that $im tau subset ker tau'$, that is, $(L_P Lambda_P) = 1$ in
  $Picent Lambda_P$ for each $L in I(C)$ and each $P$. Since $C$ is an
  $R$-submodule of $Lambda$, $C$ is itself an $R$-order in $K C$. Therefore
  $C_P$ is an $R_P$-order, whence $Picent C_P = 1$ by
  @th:picent-commutative-semilocal. Consequently $L_P = C_P u$ for some unit
  $u in (K C)_P$, and hence
  $
    (L_P Lambda_P) = (u Lambda_P) = 1 "in" Picent Lambda_P.
  $
  This proves that $im tau subset ker tau'$.

  To prove the reverse inclusion, suppose that $(X) in Picent Lambda$ is such
  that $tau'(X) = 1$, that is, $(X_P) = 1$ in $Picent Lambda_P$ for each $P$. We
  may assume that $X in I(Lambda)$, and so $X_P in I(Lambda_P)$ for each $P$. It
  follows from Exercise~@exc:bimodule-isomorphic-to-order that for each $P$
  there exists a unit $c_P in c(A_P)$ such that $X_P = Lambda_P c_P$.
  Furthermore, since $X_P = Lambda_P$ a.e., we may choose $c_P = 1$ a.e. Now set
  $
    L = K C inter.big {inter.big_P C_P c_P}, quad
    L' = K C inter.big {inter.big_P C_P c_P^(-1)}.
  $
  By @th:lattice-from-completions, $L$ and $L'$ are $C$-lattices in $K C$ such
  that
  $
    L_P = C_P c_P, quad L'_P = C_P c_P^(-1) quad "for all" P.
  $
  Therefore
  $
    L L' = K C inter.big {inter.big_P L_P L'_P}
    = K C inter.big {inter.big_P C_P} = C,
  $
  whence $L$ and $L'$ are invertible $C$-ideals in $K C$. Thus
  $(L) in Picent C$, and we need only verify that $(X) = tau(L)$. For each $P$
  we have
  $
    X_P = Lambda_P c_P = Lambda_P L_P,
  $
  and therefore
  $
    X = A inter.big {inter.big_P X_P} = A inter.big {inter.big_P (Lambda L)_P}
    = Lambda L.
  $
  This proves that $ker tau' subset im tau$, and completes the proof that
  $im tau = ker tau'$.

  _Step 5._ It remains for us to prove that $tau'$ is epic. Suppose that for
  each $P$ we are given an element $X(P) in I(Lambda_P)$, and we wish to find an
  $(X) in Picent Lambda$ such #source(342)that $tau'(X) = product X(P)$. Since
  $Picent Lambda_P = 1$ a.e., we may assume that $X(P) = Lambda_P$ a.e. Now set
  $
    X = A inter.big {inter.big_P X(P)}.
  $
  By @th:lattice-from-completions, $X$ is a two-sided $Lambda$-submodule of $A$
  such that $X_P = X(P)$ for all $P$.

  For each $P$, let $Y(P)$ be the inverse of $X(P)$, and set
  $
    Y = A inter.big {inter.big_P Y(P)}.
  $
  Then, as above, $Y$ is also a two-sided $Lambda$-submodule of $A$ such that
  $Y_P = Y(P)$ for all $P$. But then $X Y = Y X = Lambda$, since these
  equalities hold locally at all $P$. This shows that $(X) in Picent Lambda$,
  and that $tau'(X) = product X(P)$. Hence $tau'$ is epic, and the proof of
  @th:picent-local-global[Theorem] is finished.
]

The exactness of the sequence @eq:picent-local-global shows that the calculation
of $Picent Lambda$ can be carried out in two steps:
+ The determination of the group $Picent C$ for a commutative order $C$,
+ The determination of the groups $Picent Lambda_P$ for local orders $Lambda_P$.
There is the further problem of determining the structure of the extension
$Picent Lambda$, once $Picent C$ and $product Picent Lambda_P$ are known. There
are as yet no general results concerning this last question.

#corollary[
  Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain, and let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in a
  central simple $K$-algebra $A$. For each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$ dividing the
  discriminant $d(Lambda slash R)$, let $e_P$ be the ramification index of the
  skewfield part of $A_P$ over $K_P$. Then there is an exact sequence of
  commutative groups
  $
    1 -> Cl R -> Picent Lambda
    -> product_(P divides d(Lambda slash R)) bold(Z) slash e_P bold(Z) -> 1.
  $ <eq:picent-maximal-sequence>
  If $K$ is a global field, then for each $P$, $e_P$ equals the local index
  $m_P$ of $A$ at $P$.
] <cor:picent-maximal-order>

#proof[
  In this case $Lambda$ has center $R$, and $Picent R tilde.equiv Cl R$ by
  @cor:picent-ideal-classes. The group $Picent Lambda$ is abelian, since
  $I(Lambda)$ is abelian by @th:two-sided-ideals-free-group. Further, for each
  $P$ we know that $Lambda_P$ is a maximal $R_P$-order in the central simple
  $K_P$-algebra $A_P$, and $d(Lambda_P slash R_P) = {d(Lambda slash R)}_P$. It
  follows from @th:picent-complete-local that $Picent Lambda_P = 1$ whenever
  $P divides.not d(Lambda slash R)$, and that
  $Picent Lambda_P tilde.equiv bold(Z) slash e_P bold(Z)$ whenever
  $P divides d(Lambda slash R)$. Then @eq:picent-maximal-sequence is just the
  exact sequence in @eq:picent-local-global. Finally, $e_P = m_P$ when $K$ is a
  global field, by @th:local-skewfield-e-f.
]

It is of interest to compare the groups $Picent Lambda$ and $Cl Lambda$ when
$Lambda$ is a maximal order. Most of the remaining material in this section
comes from Fröhlich–Reiner–Ullom [@bib:Frohlich1974], where non-maximal orders
are also considered.

#theorem[
  #source(343)Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order, as in
  @cor:picent-maximal-order. There is a homomorphism
  $
    theta : Picent Lambda -> Cl Lambda,
  $
  given by $theta(X) = [X]$, $(X) in Picent Lambda$. If $K$ is a global field
  and $A = eichler(R)$ (see @rem:eichler-hypothesis[Remark]), then
  $
    ker theta tilde.equiv Outcent Lambda.
  $
] <th:picent-to-class-group>

#proof[
  The map $theta$ carries the bimodule isomorphism class
  $(lmodule(Lambda, X)_Lambda)$ onto the stable isomorphism class
  $[lmodule(Lambda, X)]$. Now let $(X), (Y) in Picent Lambda$; without loss of
  generality, we may assume that $X, Y in I(Lambda)$ and that $X subset Lambda$.
  Setting $T = Lambda slash X$, we obtain an exact sequence of
  $(Lambda, Lambda)$-bimodules
  $
    0 -> X -> Lambda -> T -> 0,
  $
  where $T$ is an $R$-torsion bimodule. Since $lmodule(Lambda, Y)$ is
  projective, the sequence
  $
    0 -> X times.o Y -> Y -> T times.o Y -> 0
  $
  is also exact, where $times.o$ means $times.o_Lambda$. By
  @th:separable-maximal-principal, $Y_P tilde.equiv Lambda_P$ as left
  $Lambda_P$-modules, for each $P$. Therefore
  $
    T times.o Y tilde.equiv dsum_P (T times.o Y)_P
    tilde.equiv dsum_P T_P times.o Y_P tilde.equiv dsum_P T_P tilde.equiv T
  $
  as left $Lambda$-modules. Since $lmodule(Lambda, Y)$ is projective, we may
  apply Schanuel's Lemma (see Exercise~@exc:schanuel) to the above pair of exact
  sequences, so as to deduce that
  $
    X dotplus Y tilde.equiv Lambda dotplus X times.o Y quad
    "as left" Lambda"-modules."
  $
  Hence $[X] + [Y] = [X times.o Y]$ in $Cl Lambda$, which proves that $theta$ is
  a homomorphism.

  Now let $K$ be a global field and let $A = eichler(R)$. If
  $(X) in Picent Lambda$ is such that $theta(X) = 0$, then $[X] = [Lambda]$,
  whence $X tilde.equiv Lambda$ as left $Lambda$-modules by
  @cor:stable-isomorphism-ideals. This latter condition holds if and only if
  $(X) in im omega$, by @th:left-isomorphic-invertible. Therefore
  $ker theta = im omega tilde.equiv Outcent Lambda$, as claimed, and the proof
  is done.
]

#theorem[
  Keep the above notation, and let $K$ be a global field. If $K$ is a function
  field, assume further that $A = eichler(R)$. Let $(A : K) = n^2$, and for each
  maximal ideal $P$ of $R$, let $kappa_P$ be the local capacity of $A$ at $P$,
  and $m_P$ the local index. There is a homomorphism
  $
    t_n : Cl R -> Cl_A R,
  $
  induced by mapping each $R$-ideal $J$ in $K$ onto $J^n$. Then
  $
    cok theta tilde.equiv Cl_A R slash W dot im t_n,
  $
  where $W$ is the subgroup of $Cl_A R$ generated by the classes of
  $
    {P^(kappa_P) : P divides d(Lambda slash R)}.
  $
] <th:picent-class-cokernel>

#proof[
  #source(344)We have
  $
    Cl R = I(R) slash P(R), quad Cl_A R = I(R) slash P_A (R),
  $
  in terms of the notation preceding @th:eichler-ray-classes. If no infinite
  prime of $K$ ramifies in $A$, then $P(R) = P_A (R)$ and $t_n$ is obviously a
  homomorphism. On the other hand, if some infinite prime of $K$ does ramify in
  $A$, then $n$ must be even, and hence $(R a)^n in P_A (R)$ for each
  $a in K^*$. Thus $t_n$ is a well defined homomorphism in any case.

  By @th:swan-class-group, the reduced norm map $nr_(A slash K)$ induces an
  isomorphism $nu : Cl Lambda tilde.equiv Cl_A R$. Hence
  $
    cok theta tilde.equiv Cl_A R slash {nu[X] : X in I(Lambda)}.
  $
  Each $P$ determines a unique prime ideal $frak(P)$ of $Lambda$ containing $P$,
  and these ideals ${frak(P)}$ generate $I(Lambda)$ as a free abelian group, as
  proved in @th:two-sided-ideals-free-group. Furthermore, for each $P$ we have
  $
    n = m_P kappa_P, quad nr_(A slash K) frak(P) = P^(kappa_P)
  $
  by @eq:reduced-norm-prime-ideal. Thus ${nu[X] : X in I(Lambda)}$ is the
  subgroup of $Cl_A R$ generated by all classes ${P^(kappa_P)}$, $P$ arbitrary.
  But $kappa_P = n$ except when $m_P > 1$, that is, except when
  $P divides d(Lambda slash R)$. Hence this subgroup is precisely
  $W dot im t_n$, and the theorem is proved. (Another approach is given in
  Exercise~@exc:class-group-snake)
]

We shall now compare the maps $theta, theta'$, where
$
  theta : Picent Lambda -> Cl Lambda, quad
  theta' : Picent Gamma -> Cl Gamma,
$
for the case where $Gamma$ is a full matrix ring $M_n (Lambda)$ over a maximal
order $Lambda$. Our main result is

#theorem[
  Let $Lambda$ be a maximal $R$-order in a central simple $K$-algebra $A$, where
  $K$ is a global field and $A = eichler(R)$ (see
  @rem:eichler-hypothesis[Remark]). Let $n >= 1$, and set $E = M_n (R)$,
  $Gamma = M_n (Lambda)$. We may identify $Gamma$ with $E times.o_R Lambda$.
  Then
  + There is a commutative diagram of groups, with exact rows:
    $
      #picent-class-ladder
    $ <eq:picent-class-ladder>
    where
    $
      alpha(f) = 1 times.o f, quad beta(X) = (E times.o_R X), quad
      gamma[Y] = [E times.o_R Y].
    $
  + Let
    $
      (Cl Lambda)_n = {xi in Cl Lambda : n xi = 0}, quad
      n dot Cl Lambda = {n xi : xi in Cl Lambda}.
    $ <eq:n-torsion>
    Then
    $
      ker gamma = (Cl Lambda)_n, quad
      cok gamma tilde.equiv Cl Lambda slash n dot Cl Lambda.
    $
  + #source(345)There is an exact sequence of groups
    $
      1 -> Outcent Lambda stretch(->)^alpha Outcent Gamma
      stretch(->)^zeta (Cl Lambda)_n -> cok theta -> cok theta' \
      -> Cl Lambda slash n dot Cl Lambda -> 0.
    $ <eq:outcent-cokernel-sequence>
] <th:picent-matrix-order>

#proof[
  The top row of @eq:picent-class-ladder is exact by @th:picent-to-class-group.
  Next we note that $K Gamma = M_n (A)$, which is also $eichler(R)$. Hence we
  may apply @th:picent-to-class-group to the maximal order $Gamma$, so as to
  obtain the exact sequence occurring in the bottom row of
  @eq:picent-class-ladder. It is clear that @eq:picent-class-ladder is a
  commutative diagram, since all of the maps $alpha, beta, gamma$ are induced by
  $E times.o_R dot$. This establishes assertion (i) of the theorem.

  Now let $phi : Cl Lambda tilde.equiv Cl Gamma$ be the isomorphism which occurs
  in the commutative diagram @eq:k0-morita-diagram. We claim that
  $gamma = n dot phi$. Using the notation of @eq:k0-morita-diagram, it suffices
  to prove that $mu' gamma = mu' dot n phi$. For $[Y] in Cl Lambda$, we have
  $
    mu' gamma[Y] = mu'[E times.o_R Y] = [E times.o_R Lambda] - [E times.o_R Y]
    in K_0 (Gamma),
  $
  whereas
  $
    mu' dot n phi[Y] = n(L times.o_Lambda dot) mu[Y]
    = n{[L times.o_Lambda Lambda] - [L times.o_Lambda Y]} in K_0 (Gamma).
  $
  But in $K_0 (Gamma)$ we have
  $
    [E times.o_R Lambda] = n[L times.o_Lambda Lambda], quad
    [E times.o_R Y] = n[L times.o_Lambda Y]
  $
  by Exercise~@exc:matrix-k0-multiple. This completes the proof that
  $gamma = n phi$. Since $phi$ is an isomorphism, it follows at once that
  $ker gamma = (Cl Lambda)_n$, and that
  $
    cok gamma = Cl Gamma slash n dot phi(Cl Lambda)
    tilde.equiv Cl Lambda slash n dot Cl Lambda.
  $

  Finally let us prove (iii). By Exercise~@exc:matrix-picard-iso, the map $beta$
  is an isomorphism. Hence the following diagram is commutative, and has exact
  rows:
  $
    #snake-ladder
  $
  By the "Snake Lemma" (see Exercise~@exc:snake) there exists an exact sequence
  $
    1 -> ker theta -> ker theta' -> ker gamma -> cok theta
    stretch(->)^(gamma_*) cok theta',
  $
  where $gamma_*$ is induced by $gamma$. Then
  $
    cok gamma_* = {Cl Gamma slash im theta'} slash gamma_*
    {Cl Lambda slash im theta} tilde.equiv cok gamma.
  $
  Hence the sequence
  $
    cok theta stretch(->)^(gamma_*) cok theta' -> cok gamma -> 0
  $
  is also exact. This establishes that @eq:outcent-cokernel-sequence is exact,
  and completes the proof of the theorem.
]

#corollary[
  #source(346)Let $Gamma = M_n (Lambda)$ as in @th:picent-matrix-order, and keep
  the notation and hypotheses of that theorem. Then
  + For each $f in Aut_R Gamma$, we have $f^n = (1 times.o g) eta$ for some
    $g in Aut_R Lambda$ and some inner automorphism $eta$ of $Gamma$.
  + $Out_R M_n (R) tilde.equiv (Cl R)_n$. Hence for each $f in Aut_R M_n (R)$,
    $f^n$ is an inner automorphism of $M_n (R)$.
] <cor:matrix-outer-automorphisms>

#proof[
  Let $zeta$ be the map occurring in @eq:outcent-cokernel-sequence. Since
  $Gamma$ has center $R$, we have
  $
    Autcent Gamma = Aut_R Gamma, quad
    Outcent Gamma = Out_R Gamma.
  $
  For $f in Aut_R Gamma$, let $overline(f)$ denote its image in $Out_R Gamma$.
  Then $zeta(overline(f)^n) = n dot zeta(overline(f)) = 0$, whence
  $overline(f)^n in im alpha$. Hence $f^n = (1 times.o g) eta$ for some
  $g in Aut_R Lambda$ and some $eta in In Gamma$, which proves (i).

  Now let $Lambda = R$. Then $ker theta = 1$, $cok theta = 0$, since $theta$ is
  an isomorphism. The exactness of @eq:outcent-cokernel-sequence then gives
  $
    Out_R Gamma tilde.equiv (Cl R)_n,
  $
  as claimed. The remaining assertion in (ii) is now clear, and the corollary is
  proved. The result (ii) is due originally to Rosenberg–Zelinsky
  [@bib:Rosenberg1961].
]

#remark[
  As part of the hypotheses of @th:picent-to-class-group[Theorem],
  @th:picent-matrix-order[Theorem] and @cor:matrix-outer-automorphisms[Theorem],
  we assumed that
  $
    K = "global field", quad A = eichler(R).
  $ <eq:eichler-hypothesis>
  By @cor:stable-isomorphism-ideals, these assumptions imply that
  $
    [X] = [Y] "in" Cl Lambda <=> X tilde.equiv Y "as left" Lambda"-lattices."
  $ <eq:stable-isomorphism-cancels>
  It was precisely this result, rather than @eq:eichler-hypothesis itself, which
  was needed in the proof of @th:picent-to-class-group. The other two theorems,
  @th:picent-matrix-order and @cor:matrix-outer-automorphisms, depend on
  @th:picent-to-class-group. Thus all three theorems remain valid if, in their
  hypotheses, we replace condition @eq:eichler-hypothesis by the weaker
  condition @eq:stable-isomorphism-cancels.

  It may well happen that in some cases, @eq:stable-isomorphism-cancels holds
  true but @eq:eichler-hypothesis does not. For example, the quaternion algebra
  $A$ in @eq:quaternion-maximal-order fails to satisfy the Eichler condition
  relative to $bold(Z)$; nevertheless, @eq:stable-isomorphism-cancels holds for
  the maximal $bold(Z)$-order $Lambda$ given in @eq:quaternion-maximal-order,
  since in fact we showed in §~@sec:ideal-classes that every left $Lambda$-ideal
  in $A$ is principal. Even when $K$ is not a global field, there are cases
  where @eq:stable-isomorphism-cancels is valid; an obvious example is that
  obtained by choosing $A = K$, $Lambda = R$. In fact, as we shall see in
  @th:kaplansky-commutative-cancellation, stable isomorphism implies isomorphism
  whenever $Lambda$ is a commutative order.
] <rem:eichler-hypothesis>

